+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does DST stand for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Delaware statutory trust. It is a trust that owns a building, and investors buy shares of it. The IRS lets a share be the replacement in a 1031 exchange if the trust follows the limits in Revenue Ruling 2004-86."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a DST the same as a 1031 exchange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A 1031 exchange is the tax rule, and a DST is something you can buy with it. Instead of a rental you choose and run, you buy a share of a building a sponsor runs."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the minimum investment in a DST?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually $100,000. Most of the recent DST offerings we counted in SEC filings asked $100,000 or more. A small group asked as little as $1,000."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does a DST pay?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A DST pays out the cash its building has left, less reserves, on a set schedule. The amount is not fixed. The payout falls when the rent falls or a repair comes due."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you do a 1031 exchange out of a DST?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, when the trust sells its building. Your part of the money can go into a new 1031 exchange with its own deadlines. Once a REIT has swapped your shares for partnership units, it cannot."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Chapter3 set up a DST for me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. DST shares are securities, sold by licensed securities professionals, not by a real estate brokerage. We sell the rental that pays for the exchange and time the closing to your deadlines."}}]}</script>
