"><path d="M-10.7 -8H10.7V1.2C10.7 6.4 4.8 8 0 9.6C-4.8 8 -10.7 6.4 -10.7 1.2z" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><text class="r" y="3.1" text-anchor="middle">17</text></g></g><g><g transform="translate(201.8 164.3)"><g class="k"><rect x="-110.7" y="15.3" width="221.3" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="-110.7" y="14" width="221.3" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="l" x="0" y="30" text-anchor="middle"><tspan x="0">Apartments, Carolina Forest</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(66.5 68.5)"><g class="k"><rect x="13" y="-9.4" width="187.3" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="13" y="-10.7" width="187.3" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Apartments, Clear Pond</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(82.2 366.2)"><g class="k"><rect x="13" y="-9.4" width="208.2" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#1c2028" fill-opacity=".1"/><rect x="13" y="-10.7" width="208.2" height="21.5" rx="10" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity=".9" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width=".8"/><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Homes near US 17 Bypass</tspan></text></g></g></g></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">The three home communities DSTs own in Horry County, each at its parcel's center in the county's parcel file. The trusts' 7 commercial and industrial sites are not shown. Map data: U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line; Horry County GIS.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">They include two large apartment communities, one near Carolina Forest Boulevard and one in the Clear Pond area, a residential community near the US 17 Bypass, and commercial and industrial sites. Six of the 10 trusts bought since November 2024.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">None of them is a house or a condo. A DST share would be a slice of a large building like these, not a rental of your own. If you own an apartment community or a building leased to one business, DST sponsors are among the buyers here.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="pros-and-cons"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Pros and cons">What are the pros and cons of a DST?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A DST trades control and access to your money for a rental with no work.</p><style>.c3-pc{display:grid;gap:1rem;max-width:960px;margin:1.2rem 0 1.6rem}@media (min-width:760px){.c3-pc{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-pc>div{padding:1.1rem 1.2rem .6rem}.c3-pc h3{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.15rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .8rem;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.55rem}.c3-pc,.c3-pc ul{font-size:1em}.c3-pc ul{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0}.c3-pc li{display:flex;gap:.7rem;align-items:flex-start;color:var(--muted);font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .9rem}.c3-pc li svg{flex:none;margin-top:.3rem}.c3-pc .c3-pc-do{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:500;margin-top:.2rem}</style><div class="c3-pc"><div style="background:var(--ivory);border-top:3px solid var(--brass)"><h3>Pros</h3><ul><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="11" fill="var(--brass)"/><path d="M6.2 11.4l3.1 3.1 6.5-6.7" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>The tax on the sale stays put off, as in any 1031 exchange.</span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="11" fill="var(--brass)"/><path d="M6.2 11.4l3.1 3.1 6.5-6.7" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>No tenants, repairs or managers to deal with: the trust's manager runs the building.</span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="11" fill="var(--brass)"/><path d="M6.2 11.4l3.1 3.1 6.5-6.7" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>Depreciation on your share of the building is still yours to deduct.</span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="11" fill="var(--brass)"/><path d="M6.2 11.4l3.1 3.1 6.5-6.7" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg><span>You are not personally liable on the trust's loan.</span></li></ul></div><div style="background:var(--ivory);border-top:3px solid var(--muted)"><h3>Cons</h3><ul><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M6.8 11h8.4" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span>Your money is locked in until the trust sells. <span class="c3-pc-do">Put in only money you will not need for years.</span></span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M6.8 11h8.4" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span>Commissions and fees come out of your money first.</span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M6.8 11h8.4" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span>The payout is not fixed, and it falls when the rent does.</span></li><li><svg aria-hidden="true" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M6.8 11h8.4" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span>A DST sold by a REIT can turn your shares into units that cannot go into a later 1031.</span></li></ul></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-we-do"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we do">What does Chapter3 do when you sell a Grand Strand rental for a DST?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Chapter3 sells the rental. DST shares are securities, and Chapter3 does not sell them or advise on them. Each step of the sale and the exchange, with what our agent does:</p><ol class="c3d-steps" aria-label="The sale and the exchange, step by step"><li><strong>Before you list</strong>You decide to use a 1031 exchange, and that the replacement may be a DST.<span class="we">We price the rental and set the listing date so the closing falls where your plan needs it. If a tenant is in the house, the time left on the lease changes which buyers can take it.</span></li><li><strong>The sale closes</strong>The money goes to your qualified intermediary, the company that holds it while the exchange runs, not to you. Your 45 and 180 days start that day.<span class="we">We choose a buyer who can close on the date, and give your intermediary and closing attorney the closing figures.</span></li><li><strong>By day 45</strong>You name the DST in writing, and any backups.<span class="we">If you want a rental here as a backup, we find and show you choices before day 45.</span></li><li><strong>By day 180</strong>Your share of the trust is bought. If your tax return is due sooner, that date comes first.<span class="we">If the backup rental here is the one you buy, we handle the purchase so it closes in time.</span></li><li><strong>The trust sells</strong>You exchange again, or pay the tax.<span class="we">If the next exchange is a rental on the Grand Strand, we find it.</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The sale is the part we do. One of our sales, for an owner who was done managing from far away:</p><div class="c3d-callout"><p>An agent at Chapter3 sold a tenant-occupied house for a man who had owned it for 15 years. He was moving to New York to be near his sister. He did not want to manage a rental from that distance, or pay someone else to. The lease had eight months left.</p><p>The hard part was the numbers. His cash flow was over $1,200 a month. The next owner's would be about half, because the buyer would pay today's taxes and carry a larger loan. A buyout of the tenant was the fallback if no investor came, to reach owner-occupant buyers. The agent marketed the house to our list of investors who accept modest cash flow in an appreciating area. A cash buyer closed 13 days after the listing date.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Anyone who sells you a DST share must be registered with FINRA, the SEC or the state before they can sell you anything. <a href="https://www.finra.org/investors/protect-your-money/check-registration" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FINRA's free BrokerCheck</a> lists each securities firm and broker, with their licenses and record. Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> shows what the sale leaves you before the exchange.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="dst-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">DST questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does DST stand for?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Delaware statutory trust. It is a trust that owns a building, and investors buy shares of it. The IRS lets a share be the replacement in a 1031 exchange if the trust follows the limits in Revenue Ruling 2004-86.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is a DST the same as a 1031 exchange?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. A 1031 exchange is the tax rule, and a DST is something you can buy with it. Instead of a rental you choose and run, you buy a share of a building a sponsor runs.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the minimum investment in a DST?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually $100,000. Most of the recent DST offerings we counted in SEC filings asked $100,000 or more. A small group asked as little as $1,000.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much does a DST pay?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A DST pays out the cash its building has left, less reserves, on a set schedule. The amount is not fixed. The payout falls when the rent falls or a repair comes due.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you do a 1031 exchange out of a DST?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, when the trust sells its building. Your part of the money can go into a new 1031 exchange with its own deadlines. Once a REIT has swapped your shares for partnership units, it cannot.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can Chapter3 set up a DST for me?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. DST shares are securities, sold by licensed securities professionals, not by a real estate brokerage. We sell the rental that pays for the exchange and time the closing to your deadlines.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/irb/2004-33_IRB" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Rev. Rul. 2004-86</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1031" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26 U.S.C. 1031</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8824" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Form 8824 instructions</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 409</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 559</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c006.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">S.C. Code 12-6-1150</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/accredited-investors" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC accredited investors</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/reports/rule-144-selling-restricted-control-securities" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC Rule 144</a>, <a href="https://www.finra.org/rules-guidance/notices/05-18" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FINRA Notice 05-18</a>, <a href="https://www.finra.org/investors/protect-your-money/check-registration" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FINRA check registration</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/data-research/sec-markets-data/form-d-data-sets" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC Form D data</a>. DST offerings: our count of original Form D filings, October 2025 to September 2026. Condo owners and DST parcels: our count of Horry County parcels. Not legal, tax or investment advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Three mid-rise condo buildings and three condo towers behind the beach in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane over the ocean. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 crops from its center.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/invest-strategies-dst/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-condo-buildings-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/invest-strategies-dst/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-condo-buildings-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":782,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Three mid-rise condo buildings and three condo towers behind the beach in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane over the ocean","caption":"Oceanfront condo buildings in Myrtle Beach, seen from over the ocean in 2011. More than half of Horry County's condo units have an owner whose tax-bill address is outside South Carolina.","description":"Three mid-rise condo buildings and three condo towers behind the beach in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane over the ocean","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"oceanfront, north of the Myrtle Beach Boardwalk, Myrtle Beach, South Carolina"}}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Selling a Grand Strand rental?</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us the address and your deadline. We plan the listing and the closing around your exchange, and work with your intermediary and closing attorney.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Plan my rental sale</a></div></section></main>
